</header>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <p>Ask a language model to summarize an article and you usually get stenography: a faithful relay of what the article said. Useful, and shallow. It tells you the text. It doesn't <em>read</em> it.</p>
    <p>A close reader does something a relay can't. They notice what the article circles but never says — the contradiction it leaves implicit, the question it raises and declines to answer, the machinery it gestures at and never names. That negative space is often where the actual story sits. Summary Plus is a description of how to read for it: applied to a source, it surfaces the telling absence instead of flattening it.</p>
    <p>Why it works is the part worth dwelling on. A text's silences aren't random. They sit in a measurable relationship to what the text does say — and that relationship can be approached two independent ways. This page lays out both, precisely enough to follow.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">The difference</div>
    <h2>One story, two readings</h2>
    <p>A real wire story: Mexico says two US agents killed in a crash after an anti-narcotics raid were not authorized to operate on Mexican soil — one entered as a visitor, one on a diplomatic passport, and Mexican authorities were never told. Here is a plain summary, and a reading that uses the method.</p>

    <div class="readings">
      <div class="card relay">
        <div class="head">The relay — plain summary</div>
        <div class="body">Mexico said the two U.S. federal agents killed in a car crash after an anti-narcotics raid were not authorized to operate on Mexican territory. One entered as a visitor and the other on a diplomatic passport, but neither had formal accreditation, and federal authorities were not informed of their presence. The officials were widely reported to be CIA officers and died when their vehicle skidded off a mountain road and exploded after a raid on suspected drug labs in Chihuahua.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card reading">
        <div class="head">The reading — Summary Plus</div>
        <div class="body">Mexico said the two federal agents from the United States were not authorised to operate on Mexican territory; one entered as a visitor, the other on a diplomatic passport, and federal authorities were not told they were there. The source reports the crash and the raid but does not explain how unauthorised operatives came to be conducting such activities, or whether their presence was connected to any Mexican agency, consular channel, or authorized arrangement. The implied question — how US agents operated unannounced on Mexican territory during an anti-narcotics operation — remains unanswered by Mexico's official account.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>The relay tells you what happened. The reading notices that <em>"unauthorized"</em> is doing concealed work — that the absence of any consular channel, any informant, any inter-agency arrangement is itself the story, because that absence is what "unauthorized" concretely means. Same source. Same model. The difference is that one read the negative space and one didn't.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <h3>The frame that matters</h3>
      <p>Picture a class of twenty-five. Twenty-four hand in the relay. One read for the absence — and turned in the reading. You don't need a rubric to see which paper saw more. That's the whole of it: not a higher score, but the summary that read what the others walked past.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">How it works</div>
    <h2>Two ways into the unsaid</h2>
    <p>"Negative space" has to mean something specific or it means nothing. Summary Plus grounds it in two independent operations on frozen <code>bge-large-en-v1.5</code> embeddings — both SVD readings of a text, arriving at the unsaid from different directions. A concept becomes a candidate for the negative space when one or both point at it while the summaries don't.</p>

    <div class="channel">
      <div class="ctitle">Channel C-flat <span class="tag">· the centroid raycast</span></div>
      <p>Collapse the source to a single anchor vector. Cast a ray through the vocabulary and read off the concepts that sit close to the source's center of mass but appear in none of the summaries.</p>
      <div class="chips">
        <span class="chip">feds</span><span class="chip">operatives</span><span class="chip">diplomats</span><span class="chip">consulates</span><span class="chip">informants</span><span class="chip">investigators</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="channel">
      <div class="ctitle">Channel C-spiral <span class="tag">· sentence convergence</span></div>
      <p>Don't collapse to a point. Embed each source sentence and find the concepts that several different sentences triangulate toward but none states — where the text's own structure converges and stops short.</p>
      <div class="chips">
        <span class="chip spiral">coup attempt</span><span class="chip spiral">foreign interference</span><span class="chip spiral">undocumented</span><span class="chip spiral">consulate</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>The dashed terms are what convergence surfaces that the centroid misses. The two aren't redundant: across the seven stories, the spiral reached 32 source-implied concepts the centroid didn't — triangulation across sentences sees structure a single average flattens. The aggregate insight gain from adding convergence is small (+0.12), and that's how we report it. The case for the second channel is the wider, source-grounded candidate set, not the size of that number. A third channel, Channel A, restores named facts the summaries dropped, so the reading stays anchored to the source while it reaches.</p>

    <p>None of this is computation that has to run at read time. The page describes the directions the geometry surfaces and the discipline that goes with them — the reading itself does the rest. The math is the <em>reason</em> the method works, not a command to execute.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:34px;">The guardrail it carries</h3>
    <p>An absence you have to invent is not a telling one. The reading surfaces only what the source's own facts imply and the summaries omit — it names the silence where the text genuinely points past what it states, and invents nothing. That rail is what keeps "reading deeper" from sliding into "making things up."</p>

    <div class="code-cap">the two derivations, in essence · frozen bge-large-en-v1.5</div>
    <pre><span class="c"># Channel C-flat — the centroid raycast</span>
anchor = embed(source)                  <span class="c"># one vector for the whole source</span>
sims   = vocab @ anchor                 <span class="c"># cosine to every concept</span>
flat   = [w for w in top(sims) if w not in summaries]

<span class="c"># Channel C-spiral — sentence convergence</span>
S = embed(sentences(source))            <span class="c"># one vector per sentence</span>
for concept in candidates:
    conv = count(sentence · concept &gt; 0.45 for sentence in S)
    if conv &gt;= 2 and concept not in summaries:
        spiral.append(concept)          <span class="c"># several sentences point at it; none says it</span></pre>

    <p><a href="https://eigentrace.ai/comparison_all.html" class="inline-link">Read all seven stories, every pair, side by side →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">The evidence</div>
    <h2>What a small study found</h2>
    <p>The question is whether the method actually deepens a summary, and whether it smuggles anything in to do it. Across seven real stories, with a five-model blind panel and 471 blind judgements, the method scored higher on insight than plain summarization, and the provenance audit found it stayed grounded in the source.</p>

    <div class="bars">
      <div class="bar">
        <div class="blabel">Baseline<small>plain summary</small></div>
        <div class="track"><div class="fill base" style="width:47.8%"></div></div>
        <div class="bval">2.39</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bar">
        <div class="blabel">+ Channels A·C<small>facts + centroid</small></div>
        <div class="track"><div class="fill mid" style="width:66.2%"></div></div>
        <div class="bval">3.31</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bar">
        <div class="blabel">+ Convergence<small>both derivations</small></div>
        <div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:68.6%"></div></div>
        <div class="bval">3.43</div>
      </div>
      <div class="scale"><span>0</span><span>insight, blind panel score (1–5)</span><span>5</span></div>
    </div>

    <p>Insight rose from 2.39 to 3.43 on a five-point scale — about a one-point lift over plain summarization. All five judges, scoring blind, independently ranked the Summary Plus arm above the baseline. <strong>This is a small study and it has not been peer-reviewed.</strong> Seven stories is a narrow base; the code, prompts, and raw judgements are public, so the result can be checked — or broken — for roughly the cost of the API credits. Treat the number as a finding worth replicating, not a settled fact. The full inferential statistics are in the methods note below.</p>

    <p>The provenance audit — every sentence of every summary classified by where its content comes from — is the part that matters most for honesty. On each story, the strongest summary imported zero outside analogy. It traded flat observation for grounded <em>inference</em>: reasoning about the source, not invention from outside it.</p>

    <div class="prov">
      <div class="obs">Observation · 50% in source</div>
      <div class="inf">Inference · 50% grounded</div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--ink-faint);">A representative hero summary (Mexico), by sentence-level provenance: roughly half observation, half grounded inference, with analogy and speculation at zero. The empty analogy column is the claim worth checking — the signature of reading rather than inventing.</p>

    <details>
      <summary>How it was scored, and what the stats say</summary>
      <div class="dbody">
        <p>Five frontier models formed a blind panel. Arm order was shuffled per item, no single model's score decided any summary, and generation and judging were separate passes — judges never saw which arm produced which text.</p>
        <p>The reported lift is +1.04 on the five-point scale, with a bootstrap 95% CI of [+0.90, +1.18] and a large effect size (Cohen's d ≈ 0.98). We're deliberately not leading with a p-value: on a seven-story base, an extreme p-value would overstate the case more than it informs it. The honest summary is "consistent direction, large per-item effect, small sample." The judges disagreed on absolute score levels — one scored everything low, another high — but agreed unanimously on ordering. Per-sentence provenance (observation, inference, analogy, speculation) was coded by the same panel. No model evaluated its own output as the deciding judge.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">The control</div>
    <h2>It stays quiet when nothing is buried</h2>
    <p>A method that finds hidden depth everywhere is just a machine for manufacturing it. So the run included a deliberately dry story: a freight train derailed, fourteen cars left the track, no injuries, nothing released, cause under review. There's no buried machinery in it, and an honest reading should say so.</p>
    <p>The geometry, being indiscriminate, still surfaced candidate words on the dry story — <em>skidded</em>, <em>towed</em>, <em>commuters</em>. But handed those directions, the reading declined them, because there was no inference to draw from a wheel-bearing. It stayed thin and reported the two things genuinely left open — the unresolved cause, the unstated scope of disruption — and invented nothing else.</p>

    <div class="card reading" style="margin:24px 0;">
      <div class="head">Summary Plus, on the dry story</div>
      <div class="body">A freight train derailed near Millbrook Junction; fourteen of sixty-two cars left the track, three carried industrial lubricant, none released, no injuries. A preliminary inspection pointed to a possible track-gauge irregularity, yet the cause remains genuinely unresolved, with a broken wheel-bearing and excessive speed still under consideration. Service resumed that evening. The source leaves unstated how many trips or users the nine-hour closure actually affected.</div>
    </div>

    <p>That's the property that separates a reading from a generator of false depth: the lift lands in proportion to what the text actually buried. A good deal on the Mexico story. Almost nothing here.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">Where measurement ends</div>
    <h2>The selection is the reading</h2>
    <p>One honest line about how the method is built, because it shapes what this is. We tried hard to move the selection upstream — to have the geometry itself decide, before any model reads, which surfaced concepts carry real inference and which are just topic-adjacent noise. We tested six ways: a concept's rarity, its abstractness, the spread of the sentences it triangulates, its reconstruction value against the source, its position relative to the models' shared blind spot, and the narrative's trajectory toward it.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <h3>A design fact, not a defect</h3>
      <p>None of the six separated the meaningful concepts from the noise — because the distinction isn't a property of the concept. Whether <em>"consular cover"</em> opens a real inference and <em>"skidded"</em> opens nothing depends on the source it meets and the reading that meets it. Inferential value isn't in the word; it's in the act of reading the word against the text.</p>
    </div>

    <p>So Summary Plus doesn't pre-filter. It surfaces candidates geometrically — both derivations, raw — and lets the reading be the selector. That's not a limitation worked around; it's why the method is a description rather than a filter. The page supplies the negative space and the discipline; the reading does what no upstream gate could.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">The bet</div>
    <h2>A URL that carries a method</h2>
    <p>Using a URL to carry a way of reading, rather than to point at a destination, is unusual. Normally you paste a link to <em>read</em> it, not to bring a method to bear on something else. This page is an experiment in whether that works: whether a discipline, described precisely enough, can be applied just by being referenced alongside the thing you want read.</p>
    <p>Paste it beside a story and ask for a summary that uses it, and the method gives the reading something specific to look for. Whether that's broadly useful is exactly what the small study above starts to test — and what wider replication would settle.</p>
    <p>This page is the description, in full: the omission it reads for, the two derivations it rests on, the control that keeps it from manufacturing depth, and the honest limits of the evidence. Applied to a source, it surfaces what that source implies but never states. Nothing to install, nothing to run.</p>
  </div>
</section>

